{
  "title": "Laplace Transform Tables",
  "intro": "Common Laplace transform pairs with their regions of convergence, the properties, and the one-sided rules used to solve differential equations, in one place to look things up.",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "text": "Throughout, $X(s) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t) e^{-st} dt$ (the two-sided transform used in this topic), $s = \\sigma + j\\omega$, $u(t)$ is the unit step and $\\delta(t)$ the unit impulse. As with the z-transform, the formula alone does not fix the signal: the region of convergence (ROC), a vertical strip, a half-plane or the whole plane, says which one. See The Region of Convergence (#/math/laplace-transform/region-of-convergence)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Transform pairs"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "columns": [
        "Signal $x(t)$",
        "Transform $X(s)$",
        "ROC"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "$\\delta(t)$",
          "$1$",
          "every $s$"
        ],
        [
          "$\\delta(t - T)$",
          "$e^{-sT}$",
          "every $s$"
        ],
        [
          "$u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{1}{s}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > 0$"
        ],
        [
          "$-u(-t)$",
          "$\\frac{1}{s}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s < 0$"
        ],
        [
          "$t\\, u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{1}{s^2}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > 0$"
        ],
        [
          "$t^n u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > 0$"
        ],
        [
          "$e^{-at} u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{1}{s + a}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > -\\mathrm{Re}\\, a$"
        ],
        [
          "$-e^{-at} u(-t)$",
          "$\\frac{1}{s + a}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s < -\\mathrm{Re}\\, a$"
        ],
        [
          "$t\\, e^{-at} u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{1}{(s + a)^2}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > -\\mathrm{Re}\\, a$"
        ],
        [
          "$t^n e^{-at} u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{n!}{(s + a)^{n+1}}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > -\\mathrm{Re}\\, a$"
        ],
        [
          "$e^{-a|t|},\\ a > 0$",
          "$\\frac{2a}{a^2 - s^2}$",
          "$-a < \\mathrm{Re}\\, s < a$"
        ],
        [
          "$u(t) - u(t - T)$",
          "$\\frac{1 - e^{-sT}}{s}$",
          "every $s$"
        ],
        [
          "$\\cos(\\omega_0 t)\\, u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{s}{s^2 + \\omega_0^2}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > 0$"
        ],
        [
          "$\\sin(\\omega_0 t)\\, u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{\\omega_0}{s^2 + \\omega_0^2}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > 0$"
        ],
        [
          "$e^{-at}\\cos(\\omega_0 t)\\, u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{s + a}{(s + a)^2 + \\omega_0^2}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > -a$"
        ],
        [
          "$e^{-at}\\sin(\\omega_0 t)\\, u(t)$",
          "$\\frac{\\omega_0}{(s + a)^2 + \\omega_0^2}$",
          "$\\mathrm{Re}\\, s > -a$"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "In the exponential rows a may be complex, except where a > 0 is stated; in the damped sinusoids a and the frequency are real."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "text": "For causal signals the right-sided rows are the usual one-sided table found in engineering books: the ROC is then always to the right of the rightmost pole, and the system is stable when that region includes the imaginary axis."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
