    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "One-sided transform: initial conditions"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "text": "The one-sided transform $X^+(s) = \\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t) e^{-st} dt$ starts at $t = 0^-$, just before zero, so an impulse at $t = 0$ is included. Its derivative rules bring in the starting values, which is how Solving Differential Equations (#/math/laplace-transform/differential-equations) handles an RC circuit or a mass on a spring. The initial-value rule needs $X^+(s)$ to have more poles than zeros; the final-value rule holds only when every pole of $s X^+(s)$ is in the left half-plane."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "columns": [
        "Rule",
        "Signal",
        "One-sided transform"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "First derivative",
          "$\\frac{dx}{dt}$",
          "$s X^+(s) - x(0^-)$"
        ],
        [
          "Second derivative",
          "$\\frac{d^2x}{dt^2}$",
          "$s^2 X^+(s) - s\\, x(0^-) - x'(0^-)$"
        ],
        [
          "Integral from 0",
          "$\\int_{0^-}^{t} x(\\tau)\\, d\\tau$",
          "$\\frac{X^+(s)}{s}$"
        ],
        [
          "Initial value",
          "$x(0^+)$",
          "$\\lim_{s \\to \\infty} s X^+(s)$"
        ],
        [
          "Final value",
          "$\\lim_{t \\to \\infty} x(t)$",
          "$\\lim_{s \\to 0} s X^+(s)$"
        ]
      ]
    }
